<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maha Bandula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maha Bandula was a Burmese general of the Konbaung dynasty who led campaigns in Manipur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and on the frontier of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> he commanded the Burmese armies against the British, was defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> in December 1824, and was killed at Danubyu on 1 April 1825. He is honoured in Myanmar as a national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maha-bandula.jpg" alt="Maha Bandula" width="320" height="657" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kantabon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statute_of_Maha_Bandula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maha Bandula was born Maung Yit on 6 November 1782 at Dabayin, the eldest son of a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> family. He studied at the local Buddhist monastery from the age of six, but left before he was 13 when his father died, and worked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">sesame</a> fields with his mother. He later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>, then the capital, as a low-ranking retainer of Crown Prince Thado Minsaw, became one of his bodyguards in 1806, and learned military strategy there. After a bold act in 1812 caught the notice of the new crown prince, the Prince of Sagaing, he was made a messenger of the Privy Council and in June 1813 governor of Dabayin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He first saw battle in the invasion of Manipur in 1814 and served as a regimental commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> campaigns of 1817 and 1819. When the Prince of Sagaing became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a>, he made him governor of Ahlon-Monywa and on 3 November 1819 gave him the title Maha Bandula. In December 1819, as a deputy commander, he took the nearly impregnable Manipuri fort by sending commandos over the hills at night to open its gates. From February 1821 he led 20,000 men into Assam and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrakanta-singha">Chandrakanta Singha</a> in July 1822; the British refused to surrender the king, who had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>Bandula belonged to the war party at court with Queen Me Nu and her brother, the lord of Salin. The war broke out on 5 March 1824. His forces won at Ramu in May, but he stopped short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>. When a British force of over 10,000 under General Archibald Campbell seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> on 11 May 1824, he led his army across the Arakan Yoma in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and was granted the rank Agga Maha Thenapati. On 30 November he sent about 30,000 men, only half with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, against the British lines at the Shwedagon. British shells and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congreve-rocket">Congreve rockets</a> cut them down, and only 7,000 returned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He fell back to the teak stockade at Danubyu, where a first British attack in March 1825 failed. On 1 April a mortar shell killed him as he walked the fort in full insignia under a golden umbrella, ignoring his generals' warnings. Burmese tradition credits his leadership with making this the only one of the three Anglo-Burmese wars in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> put up a real fight. A school team, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> road, park and bridge, and a navy frigate bear his name, and the army's infantry badge shows his helmet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maha Bandula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Anglo-Burmese War (1824–1826), in which the general <b>Maha Bandula</b> was killed at Danubyu in 1825, showed that Burmese armies could not match British artillery and steamships, and the wars of 1852 and 1885 were decided quickly in Britain’s favour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tabayin, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Danubyu, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.47,95.15&amp;year=1809" aria-label="Tabayin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7097 1334 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maha Bandula"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7547 1604h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7547 1604h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.47,95.15&amp;year=1809">What was here in 1809? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
